TI  - TEACHING SELECTION PRINCIPLES WITH HERD RECORDS GENERATED BY AN ELECTRONIC COMPUTER
T2  - JOURNAL OF ANIMAL SCIENCE
AB  - A selection experiment was conducted with simulated dairy herds. The underlying statistical model for a milk record included all known effects acting upon a record in a natural population of dairy cattle. The all-embracing general model defined, in addition to the statistical model for records, the probabilities of individuals entering or leaving the herd, mortality, fertility, probabilities of various injuries occurring and other vital statistics. Under the assumption of multivariate normal distribution of phenotypic observations and genotypic values a method of generating records was used which yielded variables with expected value and variance equal to the corresponding true population parameters. Fertility mortality, etc. were determined through a generation of random numbers according to given probabilities and decisions were made depending upon whether the generated random number did or did not exceed a predetermined fixed constant. A specific example is described in detail.
