TI  - AMELIORATION OF THE ADVERSE EFFECT OF A GASTROINTESTINAL CHALLENGE WITH SALMONELLA-ENTERITIDIS ON WEANLING RATS BY A YOGURT DIET
T2  - AMERICAN JOURNAL OF CLINICAL NUTRITION
AB  - The response of yogurt-fed rats and the corresponding unfermented milk-fed rats to an infectious gastrointestinal challenge was compared. After 1 (2 trials), 7 (4 trials), 14 and 21 (1 trial each) days on freeze-dried milk or yogurt diets, the rats were inoculated intra-gastrically by intubation per os with 4.5 x 1010 .+-. 0.7 colony forming units (.hivin.x .+-. SEM [standard error of the mean]) of S. enteritidis. Challenged and control rats were monitored during the 3 wk post-inoculation for  viability, weight gain, feed consumption and feed efficiency. In general, challenged yogurt-fed rats exhibited significantly superior weight gain and feed efficiency (but not feed consumption) values in wk 1 post-infection, but the differences were not significant in wk 2 post-infection. Sick milk- and yogurt-fed rats convalesced in wk 3. Pooled mortality data indicated that yogurt-fed rats survived better than milk-fed rats. Yogurt did not prevent salmonellosis per se but significantly reduced  the mortality and weight gain deceleration.
