TI  - AN EVALUATION OF THE GROWTH-PROMOTING EFFECT OF THE LACTOPEROXIDASE SYSTEM IN NEWBORN CALVES
T2  - ANIMAL PRODUCTION
AB  - Results are reported from 4 trials in which neonatal calves were given whole milk or milk substitute containing the lactoperoxidase system. Trial 1 was a pilot trial conducted in Sweden in which calves given whole milk with the lactoperoxidase system gained significantly more weight than controls (P < 0.05). Trials 2, 3 and 4 were carried out in successive years at the National Institute for Research in Dairying [England, UK], using large numbers of newborn, female Friesian calves. In trial 2 a significant positive effect of the lactoperoxidase system with whole milk on live-weight gain was established by 3 wk of age (P < 0.05) and sustained until weaning at 5 wk. There was no significant effect of the lactoperoxidase system for the milk substitute treatment. In trial 3 the whole milk comparison from trial 2 was repeated. There was a significant positive effect of the lactoperoxidase system on the live-weight change after 2 wk of age only (P < 0.05). In trial 4, 2 different methods of activating the lactoperoxidase system were compared for calves given whole milk. There was no effect of either method on the live-weight gain of the calves. As the levels of scouring were extremely low for all calves, a comparison of the 2 methods of activating the lactoperoxidase system was not justified. The combined results from trials 2, 3 and 4 confirmed that the lactoperoxidase system significantly increased the live-weight gain of calves given whole milk, despite the fact that the overall level of mortality and incidence of scouring was low in these trials.
