TI  - IMMEDIATE OR LATER FEEDING FOR PREMATURE BABIES - A CONTROLLED TRIAL
T2  - LANCET
AB  - A controlled trial was carried out in 239 premature babies to compare immediate feeding (usually within 2-3 hours of birth, using large volumes of milk) with later feeding (usually at 12 hours, using smaller volumes). The mortality in the "immediate" group was 17% and in the "later" group 6%. This difference is significant (P < 0.01). If, because of postmortem findings, certain cases in the "immediate" group are excluded, a substantial difference remains, although it does not quite reach statistical significance at the 5% level. Detailed analysis has produced no explanation for this difference other than the difference in feeding methods. Inhalation of vomit was a disturbingly frequent necrospy finding in the "immediate" group. Hyperbilirubinemia and hypoglycemia were less common in the "immediate" group. Although there are some good reasons for immediate feeding, it is not without danger. Modifications of the immediate-feeding regimen are suggested.
