TI  - Primary prevention of breast cancer: continued rise of the epidemic disease worldwide, in the 1990s, and the strategy of the proposed European joint research project
T2  - 2ND BALKAN CONGRESS OF ONCOLOGY
CP  - 2nd Congress of the Balkan-Union-of-Oncology
AB  - Breast cancer (BC) suddenly rose all over the world in the 1980s and accelerated its rise in the 1990s, affecting mainly the advanced countries of the West. With the new knowledge of a significant barrier-contraception related risk factors (condoms or withdrawal), it was postulated that the carcinogenic process in the breast could be reversed, prevented and controlled. Purpose: primary (non-chemical) prevention of BC and breast diseases in the community. The intervention trial in three culturally similar and different populations of South-East Europe, Skopje, Plovdiv, and Ioannina, during 3 years. Objectives: carrying out a community trial smd intervention with advice for and supply of non-barrier devices to the patients with breast-diseases and established BC risk factors; and assessment of the preventive impact of the intervention in the community. Aim: to investigate women of reproductive age, 18-50 years, with breast diseases (cases), along the same number of healthy women (controls). Basic strategy of BC prevention: conversion of the barrier contraception into an adequate family-planning practice with non-barrier contraceptive methods (diaphragm, pills, IUDs, rhythm, creams, tubal ligation). Investigation by: interviewing (questionnaire), examinations (mammography, ultrasonography, physical check-up, markers); establishing baseline, pre-test, data; following-up of all women for 3 years, with post-test outcomes, for comparison. Expected results: BC significantly reduced and prevented in the community, by condom-use elimination, and conversion into non-barrier fertility-control practices. Confirmation of BC as a preventable health problem.
