T2  - WORLD ASSOCIATION FOR BUIATRICS - XIX CONGRESS, VOLS 1-3: VOLS 1-2: PROCEEDINGS; VOL 3: POSTER PRESENTATION
CP  - XIX World Buiatrics Congress
AB  - On 74 dairy farms in three Canadian provinces, 1882 dairy cows were used to evaluate the efficacy of an RE-17 mutant Salmonella typhimurium core antigen vaccine (Endovac-Bovi(R), Bayer Canada) against clinical coliform mastitis. Cows were administered vaccine or placebo between 120 and 240 days of gestation according to a double-blind, formally randomized (within farm) protocol. Experimental solution was administered once and boostered at a 3-week interval. Clinical mastitis events were recorded and classified by severity. Pre-treatment milk samples were aseptically collected, frozen, and subsequently submitted for bacteriological culture. There were no differences in the overall incidence rates of clinical mastitis between vaccinated cows and cows administered a placebo solution. There was no difference in the pathogen-specific cases of mastitis between the vaccine and placebo groups. The overall undifferentiated mortality rate was significantly lower (p = 0.04) in the vaccinated cows. Specific causes of death could not, be determined from available records. Only 18 of 946 vaccinated cows died versus 32 of 936 placebo-treated cows. A subset of 34 farms had a high incidence of clinical mastitis (>15% lactational incidence). On these farms, 360 cows were administered either vaccine or placebo and boostered between 200 and 240 days of gestation. For this subset of cows, the incidence of clinical mastitis with either ''no growth'' or ''coliform'' bacteriology results was lower (p = 0.06) in the vaccinated cows (10.8%, n = 185) versus placebo cows (17.7%, n = 175). The efficacy results from this study-suggest that use of an RE-17 mutant core antigen Vaccine may be warranted in herds with a high rate of clinical mastitis; provided the vaccine is strategically administered to the dry cows in order to protect during the ''high risk'' periods.
