TI  - DETERMINING FREQUENCY OF CORONAVIRUS ISOLATED FROM CALVES WITH DIARRHEAIN ZABOL CITY
T2  - JOURNAL OF FUNDAMENTAL AND APPLIED SCIENCES
AB  - Diarrhea is a major cause of death in young calves, which leads to recession, reduces productivity and production, increases mortality and cost of medication and treatment. Rotavirus andcoronavirus are the most common viral causes of diarrhea in neonataland young calves. The present study aimed to obtain information on prevalence of calf coronavirus infection and the effect of age and gender on incidence of this disease in Sistan region. For this purpose, 72 fecal samples from calves under four months of age with diarrhea were studied. Random samples were collected from dairy farms in studied regionwithin nine months. The results were interpreted based on ELISA kit manuals. The frequency of coronavirus was 2.8% among diarrheal calves in studied region. One-day to 30-day oldcalves were more susceptible to coronavirus infections than other age groups. However, no statistically significant difference was found between different age groups (p> 0.05). In addition, coronavirus infection is more prevalent in males (6.4%) than females (0%). The results showed low prevalence of coronavirus infection in studied region. Appropriate preventive measures can be used in dairy farms to reduce the incidence of diarrhea among neonatal calves with respect to risk factors of diarrhea.
