TI  - PREVENTION OF NEONATAL DIARRHEA IN CALVES BY VACCINATION OF THEIR DAMS WITH AN ENTEROPATHOGENIC ESCHERICHIA-COLI VACCINE - A FIELD-STUDY
T2  - REFUAH VETERINARITH
AB  - Repeated outbreaks of neonatal diarrhea in calves with a mortality rate of 50-61% were experienced during 3 successive years in a dairy herd. Strains of enteropathogenic E. coli and a few cases of Salmonella were isolated from intestinal contents. Cows in this herd were thereafter vaccinated with an enteropathogenic E. coli vaccine prepared from a strain that had been isolated from 1 of the sick calves. The calves were fed colostrum from their vaccinated dams. In a preliminary trial carried out in the 1977/1978 calving season, in which only 6 cows were vaccinated, none of their calves died of diarrhea, as against 78% mortality among the 9 control calves whose dams were not vaccinated. During the 1978/1979 calving season, 49 cows were vaccinated and of the 42 normal calves born alive, no cases of death caused by diarrhea were recorded; at the beginning and end of the season the mortality rate in calves from unvaccinated cows was 56% and 29%, respectively. Anti-K99 antibody titers were found in the sera and colostra of vaccinated cows and in the sera of their calves that were given the colostrum. No such antibodies were detectable in the sera and colostra of unvaccinated cows or in the sera of their calves.
