A1  - BRIT CATTLE VET ASSOC
TI  - Analysis of Pasteurella vaccination trials
T2  - WORLD ASSOCIATION FOR BUIATRICS - XIX CONGRESS, VOLS 1-3: VOLS 1-2: PROCEEDINGS; VOL 3: POSTER PRESENTATION
CP  - XIX World Buiatrics Congress
AB  - Bovine respiratory disease remains one of the most costly disease complexes to beef production worldwide. Pasteurella haemolytica biotype A serotype 1 is well recognized as a major bacterial component associated with these losses. An effective vaccine designed to control this infection would result in substantial economic savings to the cattle industry.
   A commercial Pasteurella haemolytica bacterin - leukotoxoid (OneShot(R) - Pfizer Animal Health) was evaluated for its ability to reduce morbidity, mortality, and relapse rate of bovine respiratory disease in cattle in a commercial feedyard environment.
   Three trials included treatment groups of 1) OneShot(R) given within 1 to 14 days prior to shipment to the feedyard; 2) OneShot(R) given upon arrival at the feedyard; 3) OneShot(R) given 1 to 14 days prior to shipment and also upon arrival at the feedyard; and 4) a positive control (no OneShot(R) vaccination, but all other routine processing including vaccination for respiratory viral pathogens received by all treatment groups). An additional three trials included treatment groups of 1) OneShot(R) given upon arrival at the feedyard; and 2) positive controls. A total of 5,078 head of cattle were utilized in these six trials. They included beef type calves from Canada, Montana, Texas, and Virginia, and dairy beef calves (Holsteins) from Wisconsin.
   The data was pooled within the three trials that had the four treatment groups, and all six trials with two treatment groups. The pooling was conducted according to the procedures of Cochran, W.G. and G.M. Cox, 1966, Experimental Design, John Wiley and Sons, Inc., New York.
   The pooled data showed that OneShot(R) administered upon arrival at the feedlot reduced mortality due to respiratory disease from 1.44% in positive controls to .70% in arrival vaccinated (P = .056). No significant reduction was shown in the pooled results for morbidity or relapse rate due to respiratory disease.
   These data represent a large number of cattle across a wide geographical range of locations and different types and sources of animals within different management schemes. A consistent benefit from vaccination upon arrival with this product is demonstrated in reduced mortality. In those trials where respiratory disease mortality was much higher than the average of 1.44%, a greater reduction in mortality is seen, but is not as statistically significant simply due to less total numbers of animals.
