TI  - INFLUENCE OF NONVIABLE LACTOBACILLUS FERMENTATION PRODUCT ON ARTIFICIALLY REARED PIGS
T2  - NUTRITION REPORTS INTERNATIONAL
AB  - Two trials were conducted to determine the influence of nonviable lactobacillus fermentation product (LFP) in artificially reared pigs removed from sows at 24 to 36 h postpartum. The pigs were fed a non-medicated milk replacer for 21 days in individual cages in an environmentally controlled room. In Trial I, 5 levels (0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0 and 2.0 ml/pig per day; 8 pigs per treatment) of LFP were used to determine the effect of dosage rage on growth, feed efficiency, mortality rate, white blood cell count, hematocrit and intestinal morphology. No preferred dosage rate was observed. Trial II was conducted using 2 .times. 2 factorial design with Escherichia coli challenge (with or without) and level of LFP (0, 0.5 and 1.0 ml/pig per day) as the main effects. When pigs were 14 days old, they received an inoculum of either a broth containing E. coli (strain K88, K91; approximately 1 billion organisms for 2 days) or broth without E. coli. Pigs were sacrificed 5 days post-inoculation and 7 sections of gastrointestinal tract and feces were excised to enumerate lactobacillus and coliform populations. A LFP dose of 0.5 ml per day tended to increase gain (P = 0.08) and to suppress (P < 0.05) E. coli count in the stomach area without affecting lactobacillus populations. No differences were detected with the histopathological evaluation. By challenging the pigs with E. coli, jejunal coliform and white blood cell counts were increased (P < 0.06). These results suggest that lactobacillus fermentation product suppresses E. coli counts in the stomach and may improve gain in the artifically reared pig.
